WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.090
GABE WEISS: Hi, folks.

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:04.050
I'm Gabe Weiss, a developer
advocate for Google Cloud.

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.510
Today, I'd like to introduce
you to a new product--

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.277
Database Migration
Service, or DMS.

00:00:09.277 --> 00:00:11.610
I'm also going to walk through
a quick, simple migration

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:12.780
to Cloud SQL using it.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.740
Database migration service
makes it fast and easy

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.830
to move your MySQL or PostgreSQL
database to our managed Cloud

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.010
SQL service.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.790
DMS provides a simple,
integrated experience

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:31.920
to guide you through every
step of the migration.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.510
It's serverless,
so you don't need

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.000
to worry about managing
or monitoring instances.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.950
And continuous data
replication means

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.560
you can cut over with
minimal downtime.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.630
Whether your database
is on premise,

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.900
self-hosted in Google Cloud,
or running on another Cloud,

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.740
DMS helps you quickly unlock the
benefits of managed Cloud SQL.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.840
Now, let's walk through
the DMS experience

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.330
for a new migration job.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.690
You can access DMS from the
Google Cloud console navigation

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.730
under Databases.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.470
Just click Database Migration.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:05.050
To get started, you'll
create a migration job.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.060
This represents the
end-to-end process

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.250
of moving your source database
to a Cloud SQL destination.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:17.150
First, let's define what kind
of migration the job will run.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.720
I want to move a
MySQL database I

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.990
am running on GCE to Cloud SQL.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:28.070
I can choose between one-time
or a continuous replication.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:31.920
I'll select continuous
for minimal downtime.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.140
Once I've defined
my job, DMS shows

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.270
me the source and
connectivity configuration

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.880
required to be able to connect
to and migrate the source

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.840
database.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.780
DMS makes it easy
to know what I need

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.600
to do to prepare by showing
me the prerequisites directly

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:46.740
in the UI.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.990
It also supports the setup
of multiple networking

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:52.530
connectivity options, including
secure private connectivity

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.510
with built-in scripts
for easy execution.

00:01:57.312 --> 00:01:59.770
If I need to make changes to
the configuration of my source

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.680
database, I can click
Save and Exit to pick up

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:03.700
where I left off later.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.350
I'm ready to define my source.

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:11.717
I can do this by creating a
connection profile, a resource

00:02:11.717 --> 00:02:13.300
that represents the
information needed

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.110
to connect to a database.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:17.920
These profiles aren't locked
to an individual migration.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.500
This means I can reuse it
if I want to first test out

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.140
a migration or if someone
else in my organization

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:27.170
is in charge of connecting
to the database.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.400
I have already created
a connection profile

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.447
for a MySQL database running
on GCE, so I'll select it.

00:02:34.447 --> 00:02:36.780
Now I'm ready to create the
Cloud SQL instance that I'll

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.047
migrate my database to.

00:02:39.047 --> 00:02:41.380
This will feel familiar to
anyone that's created a Cloud

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:43.370
SQL instance before.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:44.870
You'll see many of
the same options,

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:49.970
like connectivity and
machine configuration.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.025
Since DMS relies on the
replication technology

00:02:52.025 --> 00:02:55.670
of the Database Engine, I don't
have to create resources, aside

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.590
from the Cloud SQL
instance I've just defined.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.800
I'm ready to define how my
new instance will securely

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.960
connect to my source
database for continuous data

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.190
replication.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:08.610
I have a few options.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:11.210
I can choose to allowlist the
IP address of the Cloud SQL

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:14.105
destination on the
database sources machine.

00:03:14.105 --> 00:03:16.130
I can set up
private connectivity

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:19.490
through a reverse SSH tunnel
on a VM in my project.

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.920
Or I can set up VPC peering.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.350
Since my source is already
running on Google Cloud,

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:25.580
I'll choose VPC peering.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:31.940
And that was easy.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.430
I've configured my source,
created a Cloud SQL

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:37.730
destination, and established
connectivity between them.

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:40.160
All that's left is to validate
the migration job setup

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.677
and start my migration.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:49.460
My migration job validation
passed successfully.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:52.480
I can trust that my
migration will run smoothly.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.250
I'll create and start the
job to run it immediately.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:57.810
Once my migration
job has started,

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.460
I can monitor its
progress and see

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.620
if it encounters any issues.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.810
DMS will first transfer
the initial snapshot

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:07.290
of existing data, then
continuously replicate changes

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:08.910
as they happen.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.920
And since DMS is
serverless, I never

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.800
have to worry about the
migration resources themselves.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:14.910
That's it.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.010
I've quickly and easily
shown you one of the ways

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:20.970
to migrate a MySQL database out
of GCE and into Cloud SQL using

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.019
DMS.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:24.210
When the initial snapshot's
migrated and continuous

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:27.060
replication is keeping up,
I can promote Cloud SQL

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.460
to be my primary instance
and point my applications

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.725
to work directly against it.

00:04:32.725 --> 00:04:34.600
Google Cloud's new
database migration service

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.640
is the fast, easy,
and reliable way

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:40.120
to migrate with minimal downtime
to a managed Cloud SQL database

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:42.120
for both MySQL and PostgreSQL.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.830
It's serverless, high
performance, and free to use.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:46.767
And now it's your
turn to give it a try.

00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:48.850
You can get started from
the Google Cloud console,

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:51.370
or learn more by following
the links in the description.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.250
Thanks for watching.

